GPL status
----------
The compiled vectra-controller-pro binary includes no GPL-licensed source code,
and this package ships no GPL-derived files at all.

Unlike the legacy vectra-controller-agent, which shipped one GPL-derived Lua
helper (passwall-component-update.lua) as data, vectra-controller-pro ships no
.lua files and embeds none. It is the xray-direct controller: it does not drive
PassWall2's built-in component updater, and it reimplements PassWall2's
subscription and route-generation behaviour natively in Go
(internal/routepolicy, internal/subscription). Where the source references
PassWall2's own files — util_xray.lua, subscribe.lua, rule_update.lua, api.lua
— those are the router firmware's own, separately installed GPL-3.0 packages
(upstream Openwrt-Passwall/openwrt-passwall2) that this controller invokes as
external commands or reimplements; none of their code is included here. The
parity comments in internal/routepolicy and internal/subscription describe that
native reimplementation; they are not included GPL code.

Other software on the router
----------------------------
The package's runtime dependencies (procd, uci, jshn, jsonfilter and others;
the LuCI surface adds luci-base and rpcd), and PassWall2 where it is installed,
are separate packages. None of their code is included in this package. Vectra's
code starts them as commands, reads and writes their configuration through the
uci tool, and exchanges data with them over ubus and rpcd.

Like every OpenWrt service, the package's shell scripts also run on the
standard OpenWrt platform shell interfaces provided by the device firmware:
the init scripts run under /etc/rc.common, and the render helpers source
/lib/functions.sh and /usr/share/libubox/jshn.sh. Those files are part of the
firmware and are not included in this package. The data plane itself is Xray,
a separate package installed on the router; the controller runs it as a command
and never includes its code.

